  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "Let \\(n\\) be the smallest nonprime integer greater than \\(1\\) with no prime factor less than \\(10.\\) Then",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(100\\lt n\\le110\\)",
    "B": "\\(110\\lt n\\le120\\)",
    "C": "\\(120\\lt n\\le130\\)",
    "D": "\\(130\\lt n\\le140\\)",
    "E": "\\(140\\lt n\\le150\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The smallest allowed prime factor is \\(11\\). Since \\(n>1\\) is not prime, it is composite, so write \\(n=ab\\) for integers \\(a,b>1\\).\n\nBoth factors must be at least \\(11\\). Indeed, any integer from \\(2\\) through \\(10\\) has a prime divisor among \\(2,3,5,7\\); if such an integer divided \\(n\\), that smaller prime would also divide \\(n\\). Therefore\n\\[\nn=ab\\ge11\\cdot11=121.\n\\]\nThis bound is attained by \\(121=11^2\\), whose only prime factor is \\(11\\). Hence the smallest qualifying integer is \\(\\boxed{121}\\), and \\(120<121\\le130\\), choice C.",
